  "body": " Thunderstorm Distance & 30\/30 Lightning Safety Warning   In Chapter 2, you calculated the distance of a lightning strike based on the air temperature in Celsius and the number of seconds until thunder is heard. The National Weather Service promotes the 30\/30 Rule: seek shelter immediately if the flash-to-bang time indicates lightning is within 6 miles.  Write a program that prompts the user for the ambient air temperature in Celsius and the elapsed time in seconds between seeing lightning and hearing thunder.     Input Validation ( if-else ): Check that the air temperature is between and inclusive, and that seconds is non-negative ( ). If invalid, display Invalid sensor inputs. and exit.    Calculations: Compute speed of sound ( ), distance in meters ( ), and distance in miles ( ).    Internal State Assertion ( assert ): Insert an assertion verifying that the calculated speed of sound is positive ( assert soundSpeed > 0 : \"Calculated non-positive sound speed\"; ).    Multi-Way Safety Zone ( if-else if-else ):   Distance : IMMINENT DANGER: Strike is within 1 mile! Seek fully enclosed indoor shelter immediately.  Distance and : WARNING: Strike is within 6 miles (30\/30 rule). Suspend outdoor activities.  Distance : ADVISORY: Distant storm; monitor weather closely.     Ternary Singular\/Plural Formatting ( ?: ): Display the elapsed seconds using the ternary operator so 1.0 second prints as 1.0 second while other values print as X.X seconds .      Testing Requirement: Create a test plan table with test cases verifying the 1.0-mile boundary, the 6.0-mile boundary, zero seconds (an immediate strike), and negative inputs.  Here is an example of the program running:   22.0  4.5  === Thunderstorm Proximity & Safety Report === Speed of Sound : 344.6 m\/s Observed Delay : 4.5 seconds Strike Distance : 1,550.8 meters (0.96 miles) Safety Status : IMMINENT DANGER: Strike is within 1 mile! Seek fully enclosed indoor shelter immediately.    "
