  "title": "Additional variable roles",
  "body": " Additional variable roles        Role name  Description & purpose  How its value changes  Java example    Fixed value  Represents a quantity that does not change after its initial assignment.  Set once (by literal, input, or calculation) and remains constant throughout its scope.   final double PI = 3.14159;  int targetNumber = (int)(Math.random() * 10) + 1;     Follower  Tracks the previous value of another variable, always trailing it by one step.  Updated immediately before or after the tracked variable moves to a new value.   Tracking consecutive duplicates or change over time:  int previousGuess = userGuess;  userGuess = stdin.nextInt();     One-way flag  A boolean variable that records whether a specific state or event has occurred.  Starts with an initial state (usually false ) and changes at most once (to true ), never reverting back.   In search or prime-checking loops:  boolean found = false; if (guess == magicNumber) { found = true; }     Temporary  Holds a value for a very short duration, usually across just two or three lines of code.  Assigned immediately before it is used and discarded immediately afterward.   Classic 3-line variable swap:  int temp = a; a = b; b = temp;      Walker  (Revisit after Chapter 6)   Moves through a data structure (such as an array), but its movements depend on the structure’s contents rather than a fixed step.  Successive values depend on the data structure being traversed.   Jumping through a String from one delimiter to the next:  int index = text.indexOf(' '); while (index != -1) { \/\/ Process word... index = text.indexOf(' ', index + 1); }      Organizer  (Revisit after Chapter 6)   A composite collection (like an array) whose elements are rearranged or sorted.  The variable’s identity doesn’t change, but its internal elements are reordered.   Imagine int[] numbers = {5, 2, 8, 1}; is reordered in place as the loop executes.     "
