  "url": "sec-4-13-exercises.html#project-multi-batch-bakery",
  "type": "Exercise",
  "number": "4.13.1",
  "title": "Multi-Batch Bakery Production Log &amp; Dough Scaler.",
  "body": " Multi-Batch Bakery Production Log & Dough Scaler   In Chapter 3, you validated batch parameters and provided handling advice for a single batch of artisan sourdough. In commercial bakeries, however, bakers schedule an entire morning's production run consisting of multiple dough batches of varying weights and hydration levels.  Write a program that processes an entire production schedule, either entered interactively using a sentinel-controlled loop or read from a text file named baking_schedule.txt .   Program Requirements:    Input Options: Allow the user to process batches in a loop. For each batch, the program reads the dough name (a String ), the desired total dough weight in grams (a double ), and the target hydration percentage (a double , e.g., 65.0% to 82.0%). If reading interactively, use a batch name of \"DONE\" (or a weight of -1 ) as the sentinel to terminate data entry.    Input Validation: Use a validation loop ( do-while or while ) for interactive input to ensure batch weights are between and inclusive, and hydration is between and inclusive.    Ingredient Calculations: Standard sourdough uses 2.0% salt and 1.5% yeast relative to flour (100%). For each batch, calculate:     Loop Variable Roles:   Stepper: Count the total number of batches scheduled ( batchCount ).  Gatherers: Accumulate the grand total weight of flour ( totalFlour ), water ( totalWater ), and total dough mixed ( totalDough ).  Most-Wanted Holder: Track the batch with the highest hydration level ( maxHydration ) and its associated name ( maxHydrationName ).     Assertions & Verification: Document the loop invariant and use an assertion to verify that totalDough upon loop exit matches the sum of total flour, water, salt, and yeast within an acceptable floating-point tolerance ( ).    File Output: Using PrintWriter , generate an itemized production report saved to daily_bake_report.txt formatted with aligned columns.      Testing Requirement: Construct a loop test plan table that tests:  Zero batches (immediate sentinel entered on first prompt).  Exactly one batch.  Multiple typical batches.  Boundary hydration values ( and ).    Here is an example of the program running:   === Artisan Bakery Production Planner ===  Country Loaf  3000.0  75.0    Baguettes  1800.0  66.0    Ciabatta  2400.0  82.0    DONE  ============================================================ BAKERY PRODUCTION SUMMARY (3 Batches) ============================================================ Total Dough Mixed : 7,200.0 g Total Flour Required : 4,136.2 g Total Water Required : 3,063.8 g Highest Hydration : 82.0% (Ciabatta) Average Batch Size : 2,400.0 g ============================================================ Report successfully saved to 'daily_bake_report.txt'.    "
